From: postmarketOS rhodep port
Subject: [PATCH] arm64: dts: qcom: sm6375-motorola-rhodep: enable charger I2C buses

This device has no battery reporting at all: /sys/class/power_supply is
empty, so userspace cannot tell whether it is charging or how much charge
is left.

Unlike most Qualcomm phones, rhodep does not use the PMIC charger block.
Charging is done by discrete I2C chips, and the vendor tree declares
several alternatives on two QUP buses (downstream naming in parentheses):

  qupv3_se8_i2c  (QUP1 SE2, our i2c8)  sgm,sgm41542   @ 0x3b
                                       ti,bq2589x-1   @ 0x6a
                                       cellwise,cw2217 @ 0x64
                                       sm,sm5602      @ 0x71
  qupv3_se10_i2c (QUP1 SE4, our i2c10) ti,bq25970     @ 0x66

All of them are marked "okay" downstream because a single vendor driver
probes the bus and keeps whichever chip answers; Motorola shipped more
than one hardware variant under the same DTS. So the device tree cannot
tell us which charger and which fuel gauge this unit actually has, and
picking the wrong compatible would either do nothing or poke registers on
the wrong chip.

Enable the two buses on their own first, with no children. That makes the
hardware enumerable from userspace (i2cdetect) so the populated addresses
can be identified before any charger or fuel gauge node is committed.

Both QUP wrappers and both GPI DMA engines are already enabled, and
sm6375.dtsi already carries the matching pinctrl states, whose pins agree
with the vendor tree (gpio19/20 for i2c8, gpio4/5 for i2c10). Enabling an
I2C controller with no children is harmless: without a driver bound to a
child, nothing drives the bus.

--- a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/qcom/sm6375-motorola-rhodep.dts
+++ b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/qcom/sm6375-motorola-rhodep.dts
@@ -169,6 +169,21 @@
 	status = "okay";
 };
 
+/*
+ * Charger / fuel gauge buses. Left childless on purpose: see commit message.
+ * i2c8  = QUP1 SE2, pins gpio19/gpio20 (vendor qupv3_se8_i2c)
+ * i2c10 = QUP1 SE4, pins gpio4/gpio5   (vendor qupv3_se10_i2c)
+ */
+&i2c8 {
+	clock-frequency = <400000>;
+	status = "okay";
+};
+
+&i2c10 {
+	clock-frequency = <400000>;
+	status = "okay";
+};
+
 &pmk8350_adc_tm {
 	status = "okay";
 };
